Why Stacking Works - and When It Doesn't
======================================

Open index.html directly (file://), or serve this directory with its sibling
TeachingTools directory. No R, Shiny, WebR, external library, build, install or
network connection is needed for the HTML lesson. app.R and model.R remain
unchanged and available as the original R implementation.

Lecture:
https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section2c_html.html

Explore
-------
Choose exponential observations, a 95/5 Gaussian mixture with rare bright
outliers, or standard Cauchy errors. Controls retain 1-100 measurements per
average and 200-3000 independent experiments. The original guided examples
(one measurement, large stack, Cauchy counterexample) and lecture guidance
are retained. Rewind/play grows each independent stack using its cached
sequence. Explain describes assumptions, equations and tail truncation.

For exponential and mixture observations the sampling plot shows
sqrt(n)*(average-mu)/sigma against Normal(0,1). Their moments are known:
(mu, variance) = (1,1) and (0.25,2.1875), respectively. The raw average has
SD sigma/sqrt(n); the CLT is asymptotic, not exact at small n.

Cauchy population mean and variance are undefined. Its raw averages are
exactly standard Cauchy for every n, with population IQR 2. The page never
standardises them using invented moments. The displayed finite-run IQR is
an empirical summary, not a population moment or a standard error.

Intentional HTML differences
---------------------------
* Uses Astro.createRng(seed), not R's RNG: equal seed numbers produce
  different individual draws, with the same statistical laws.
* Caches 3000 independent sequences of length 100, plus 8000 separate
  observations for the population illustration. Changing n or experiment
  count selects stable prefixes; playback and resizing never resimulate.
  At most one seed/population cache is retained by the UI.
* Uses 100 percentage frames rather than R's 60; frame zero means one
  measurement, not an empty average. Starts paused at the final frame.
* Adds a finite-run raw-average IQR and analytic population curve.
  Histograms retain all observations in their denominator even when the
  fixed plotting window excludes tails; excluded counts are visible.
* Seed supports the shared browser range 0 through 2147483647.

Model API
---------
model.js is pure browser/CommonJS code (CentralLimitModel in the browser):
  populations
    Metadata: mean, variance (both null for Cauchy), limits, label.
  validate(parameters)
    Checks seed, population, n and repetitions; throws Astro.ValidationError.
  populationDensity(population, x)
    Analytic density of an individual observation.
  prepare(seed, population)
    Returns seed, population, observations and a 300000-element Float64Array
    of cumulative sums, indexed experiment*100 + (n-1).
  compute(parameters, prepared?)
    Validates settings and an optional matching cache; returns data,
    a parameters snapshot and population metadata without drawing a frame.
  atFrame(result, frame=100)
    Returns n, means, statistic, finite, meanSd and empiricalIqr.
    Cauchy meanSd is null and statistic is the unstandardised means.

Files and validation
--------------------
Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, plus
../TeachingTools/{statistics.js,browser.js,browser.css,lesson.js}.
No additional runtime assets are required. README and test files are not
part of the seven-file runtime.

From the repository root, with Node 22+ and Chrome installed:
  node --test CentralLimit/test_model.js
  env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/CentralLimit" node CentralLimit/test_browser.js

Numerical tests cover known moments, exact synthetic stack arithmetic,
finite-variance standardisation, nonshrinking Cauchy quantiles, cached
prefixes, omitted tails and invalid settings. The shared real-browser
runner plus custom assertions cover all populations/control boundaries,
visible Canvas changes, new-realisation stability, playback, presets,
reset, validation, accessible tabs, mobile layout and offline file loading.
